        onChange={setDraggingSeconds}
        onCommit={(seconds) => {
          setDraggingSeconds(null);
          onSeek(seconds * 1000);
        }}
        min={0}
        max={durationSeconds}
        step={1}
        label="Seek"
        disabled={disabled || durationSeconds === 0}
      />

  …with `const [draggingSeconds, setDraggingSeconds] = useState<number | null>(null);` added to
  `Scrubber`, and `formatClock` fed the same fallback so the elapsed readout follows the thumb
  during a drag: `formatClock(draggingSeconds !== null ? draggingSeconds * 1000 : positionMs)`.

  Cost: `Scrubber` stops being purely stateless (it holds one local drag value), `Slider` gains
  one optional public prop, and the audio does not move until the user releases — which is the
  standard behaviour of a media scrubber but IS a behaviour change from scrub-to-hear.

OPTION B — leave it as written (status quo).

  Keep continuous seeking. Cost: a drag issues one worker seek plus one sample reset per pointer
  move, and re-arms the post-seek guard on each one. Benefit: the code stays exactly as planned,
  `Scrubber` stays stateless, and the user hears audio scrub past as they drag — which some
  practice tools deliberately offer. This may be acceptable for v0 on a fast machine and is the
  cheapest option today.

OPTION C — keep `onChange`-only wiring but throttle the seek in `PlayerShell`.

  Leave both `client/` components untouched and coalesce inside `seek` (one seek per animation
  frame, same shape as finding L2-F6). Cost: the design-system component keeps a shape that
  pushes the problem onto every future consumer (Plan C's settings rows and per-track volume
  sliders will each need their own throttle), and the throttle is invisible from the component
  that causes it. Benefit: no public API change, no new state in `Scrubber`, and a drag still
  scrubs audibly, just ~6x less often.
